<!--
  ALMOST

  Instance: 1000 x 700 mm, intended viewing distance 1500 mm.
  Units are millimetres of the physical output. 1:1.

  Every mark in this piece is specified in ARCMINUTES, not millimetres.
  The work has a fixed size on the retina; only the paper changes.

      gap             0.5'
      line weight     0.8'
      dot diameter    8.0'

  At 1500 mm one arcminute subtends 0.436332 mm, giving

      gap             0.218166156 mm
      stroke          0.349065850 mm
      dot radius      1.745329252 mm

  Human gap-detection threshold sits near one arcminute. The gap here is
  half that: not invisible, but below the point at which the eye can
  commit. You cannot resolve whether they touch. You can only decide.

  Because the gap is angular, it cannot be scaled. Printing this file at
  three metres wide does not produce this work â€” it produces a dot
  floating above a line. Each format is a different file. Use the
  generator to derive the correct instance for a given size and distance.

  The line runs from -20 to 1020: it bleeds past both trims at any crop.
  It is a surface, not an object. It does not end. Only the dot is
  withheld.

  Render with antialiasing. shape-rendering="crispEdges" will snap the
  gap out of existence and destroy the piece.

  On a screen the gap is narrower than a pixel, and antialiasing paints
  the seam a grey that is the area-weighted average of ink and paper.
  The screen cannot show almost-touching, so it shows the mean of
  touching and not. It answers the question the print refuses to ask.
  The work is the print; the screen is its average.

  No wall text. Title only.

  COLOPHON
-->
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
     viewBox="0 0 1000 700"
     width="1000mm" height="700mm"
     shape-rendering="geometricPrecision">

  <title>Almost</title>
  <desc>A horizontal black line running off both edges of a white field. A small black dot hovers directly above its centre, separated by half an arcminute â€” a gap too small for the eye to confirm.</desc>

  <rect x="-1" y="-1" width="1002" height="702" fill="#ffffff"/>

  <line x1="-20" y1="420" x2="1020" y2="420"
        stroke="#000000"
        stroke-width="0.349065850"/>

  <circle cx="500" cy="417.861971666307" r="1.745329252"
          fill="#000000"/>

</svg>
